      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-13288",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it gives an instruction.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14037109/example-13288.json",
          "file_sha256": "6fac0a75faebfd12241d37d7238c70001da576da2373770e4717a78b88dc9252"
